.\" robodr-kafka-publish(1). Source of truth: the USAGE text in
.\" crates/robodr-export/src/bin/robodr-kafka-publish.rs and the crate README.
.\" Keep them in sync.
.TH ROBODR\-KAFKA\-PUBLISH 1 "2026-10-16" "RoboDR 0.1.0-alpha.1" "RoboDR Manual"
.SH NAME
robodr\-kafka\-publish \- publish RoboDR findings to the robot\-edr.findings Kafka topic
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B robodr\-kafka\-publish
.B \-\-bootstrap
.I HOST:PORT
.RB [ \-\-bootstrap
.IR HOST:PORT ]...
.B \-\-state\-dir
.I DIR
.RB ( \-\-diode\-out
.I DIR
|
.B \-\-spool
.IR DIR )
.RB [ \-\-topic
.IR T ]
.RB [ \-\-chunk
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-client\-id
.IR S ]
.RB [ \-\-request\-timeout\-ms
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-delivery\-timeout\-ms
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-retries
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-max\-request\-bytes
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-interval\-ms
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-once " | " \-\-check ]
.RB [ \-\-upgrade\-data\-source
.IR LABEL ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-idempotence ]
.RB [ \-\-dead\-letter\-max\-bytes
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-dead\-letter\-keep
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-dead\-letter\-max\-age\-s
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-done\-max\-age\-s
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-done\-max\-count
.IR N ]
.RB ( \-\-tls\-ca
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-tls\-cert
.I FILE
.B \-\-tls\-key
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-tls\-server\-name
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-tls\-spki\-sha256
.IR HEX ]...
.RB [ \-\-sasl\-mechanism
.I M
.B \-\-sasl\-username
.I U
.B \-\-sasl\-password\-file
.IR FILE ]
|
.BR \-\-allow\-plaintext )
.SH DESCRIPTION
SOC\-side (high side) publisher. It reads either the
.BR robodr\-diode\-unpack (1)
importer's
.IB DIR /<batch>/records.jsonl
.RB ( \-\-diode\-out )
or a connected site's
.BR robodr\-agentd (8)
spool
.RB ( \-\-spool ),
re\-validates every finding, requires its key and headers to be exactly those
derived from the value (contract \(sc2), computes the partition from the robot
id and the topic's current partition count (contract \(sc4) and produces the
record value byte for byte. An optional per\-finding signature header
.B robodr\-finding\-sig
(contract \(sc2a) must be the last header; it is shape\-checked and forwarded
verbatim, for the consumer to verify. Invalid or broker\-rejected records go to
a dead\-letter file. A broker refusing the record timestamps
(INVALID_TIMESTAMP) is not blamed on the records: every record carries this
host's clock as its CreateTime (Kafka 4.0 refuses records more than 1 h ahead
by default), so the run fails with a hint to check the clock (NTP), nothing
is dead\-lettered or committed, and it is retried. A spool file that cannot
be read (I/O error such as EACCES or EIO, 3 attempts) is dead\-lettered with
the error and moved to the spool's rejected/ (kept for a re\-drive), counted
as
.BR quarantined_unreadable ,
and the run goes on; progress (committed line count per batch, done markers)
is replaced atomically after each acknowledged chunk.
.PP
Producer settings: acks=all, idempotent (InitProducerId, a producer id and
per\-partition sequence numbers: a batch re\-sent after a lost acknowledgement
is dropped by the broker; the producer id, sequences and the chunk's batch
journal are saved in the progress file before each request, so a chunk
re\-sent after a crash is not duplicated while the broker remembers the
producer id, by default one day), one request in flight, no compression.
With
.B \-\-no\-idempotence
delivery is at\-least\-once; consumers drop duplicates on
.BR event\-uid .
TLS is required: every broker connection, bootstrap and advertised, is TLS
and the broker certificate is verified against
.B \-\-tls\-ca
(chain, validity, serverAuth and the broker's host name as dialled). Plain TCP
only with an explicit
.BR \-\-allow\-plaintext .
Verified against single\-node Apache Kafka 3.9.1 and 4.1.0 brokers
(\fBscripts/kafka\-it.sh\fR) and, for TLS, mutual TLS and SCRAM, 3.9.1
(\fBscripts/kafka\-tls\-it.sh\fR). Pointed at a TLS listener in plain TCP,
or at a SASL listener without SASL, it fails with an error that says so.
.PP
Without
.B \-\-once
it publishes every
.B \-\-interval\-ms
and retries after an error; each step that published, dead\-lettered or
finished a batch prints a JSON report on stdout.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-bootstrap " HOST:PORT"
Bootstrap broker (repeatable, at least one).
.TP
.BI \-\-state\-dir " DIR"
Progress, done markers and dead letters.
.TP
.BI \-\-diode\-out " DIR\fR | " \-\-spool " DIR"
The input (exactly one).
.TP
.BI \-\-topic " T"
Topic (default
.BR robodr.findings ).
.TP
.BI \-\-chunk " N"
Records per produce request (default 500).
.TP
.BI \-\-client\-id " S"
Kafka client id (default
.BR robodr\-kafka\-publish ).
.TP
.BI \-\-request\-timeout\-ms " N\fR, " \-\-delivery\-timeout\-ms " N"
Per\-request deadline (default 35000; it must exceed the broker\-side produce
timeout, 30000) and total delivery deadline per chunk
(default 120000).
.TP
.BI \-\-retries " N"
Retries of retriable errors (default 10, exponential backoff).
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-request\-bytes " N"
Largest produce request (default 1 MiB).
.TP
.BI \-\-interval\-ms " N"
Loop interval, > 0 (default 2000).
.TP
.B \-\-once
One pass, then exit.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Site acceptance: the state and input directories exist, the cluster
answers with this TLS (CA, client certificate, pins, CRLs) and SASL
configuration, and the topic's metadata (partitions) is readable. Nothing
is produced and nothing written; bounded to 30 s. Exit 0 passed, 1 failed.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-idempotence
Producer id \-1, no sequence numbers: for brokers that refuse InitProducerId
to this principal (before Kafka 3.0, the IDEMPOTENT_WRITE cluster ACL is
needed; 3.0 and later imply it from WRITE on a topic). Without this flag
such a refusal is a fatal error that names the ACL.
.TP
.BI \-\-dead\-letter\-max\-bytes " N\fR, " \-\-dead\-letter\-keep " N"
Opt in to rotation of the dead\-letter file: rotate at N bytes (at least
64 KiB; default 0 = never: one growing file, nothing ever deleted) and keep N
rotated files (default 4;
.B \-\-dead\-letter\-keep
needs
.BR \-\-dead\-letter\-max\-bytes ).
A dead letter may be the only copy of a finding Kafka refused, so the
default keeps them all; monitor the file's size. With rotation, a rotated
file beyond them is DELETED once none of its entries is still needed for
deduplication (its diode batch has a done marker or left the importer
output; its spool file left ready/); each deletion of a file that held
entries is logged on stderr with its entry count and reported as
.B pruned_dead_letters.removed_entries
in the JSON report.
.TP
.BI \-\-dead\-letter\-max\-age\-s " N"
Also delete rotated dead\-letter files older than N seconds (same rule; 0 or
absent = no age bound).
.TP
.BI \-\-done\-max\-age\-s " N\fR, " \-\-done\-max\-count " N"
Delete done markers older than N seconds (default 2592000, 30 days; 0 = keep)
or beyond the N newest (default no bound), after each run. A marker is never
deleted while its batch is still in the importer output (it would be
published again): prune that output and the markers follow.
.TP
.BI \-\-tls\-ca " FILE"
PEM CA bundle: the only trust anchors for the brokers' certificates (no
system roots).
.TP
.BI \-\-tls\-cert " FILE" " \-\-tls\-key " FILE
Client certificate chain and key (mutual TLS). The key file must not be
readable by others or writable by the group.
.TP
.BI \-\-tls\-server\-name " NAME"
Name sent as SNI and verified in every broker certificate instead of the
dialled host (a DNS name or an IP literal).
.TP
.BI \-\-tls\-spki\-sha256 " HEX"
SHA\-256 of a broker's SubjectPublicKeyInfo (64 hex digits, repeatable),
checked in addition to the chain.
.TP
.BI \-\-sasl\-mechanism " M" " \-\-sasl\-username " U " \-\-sasl\-password\-file " FILE
SASL/SCRAM after the TLS handshake:
.B SCRAM\-SHA\-256
or
.BR SCRAM\-SHA\-512 .
The password is the file's content (one trailing newline removed; mode as
for the key). Needs
.BR \-\-tls\-ca .
An authentication failure is not retried.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-plaintext
Plain TCP to the brokers (an isolated SOC network or a local TLS proxy
only). Refused together with
.BR \-\-tls\-ca .
.TP
.BI \-\-upgrade\-data\-source " LABEL"
An unsigned contract 1.x record (written before an upgrade) is upgraded to
contract 2.0 with this
.B data_source
(default
.BR robodr )
and the publisher's topic, keeping its identity, and sent with headers derived
from the upgraded value. A signed 1.x record cannot be upgraded (its signature
covers the 1.x bytes) and is dead\-lettered with the reason
"legacy 1.x signed record: re\-sign required".
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success
.RB ( \-\-once ).
.TP
.B 1
Error with
.B \-\-once
(without it, errors are printed and retried).
.TP
.B 2
Usage or configuration error (also: neither
.B \-\-tls\-ca
nor
.BR \-\-allow\-plaintext ).
.SH SYSTEMD UNIT
The package ships
.B robodr\-kafka\-publish.service
(installed disabled) for a connected site: it runs on the unit or sensor box
as user
.B robodr
with
.B \-\-spool /var/lib/robodr/spool/agentd
and state in
.IR /var/lib/robodr/export/kafka\-publish ;
bootstrap brokers and extra flags come from
.I /etc/robodr/kafka\-publish.env
.RB ( ROBODR_KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP ,
.BR ROBODR_KAFKA_PUBLISH_ARGS ).
The unit denies every IP address; the drop\-in example
.I 50\-brokers.conf.example
allows the brokers. List every broker of the cluster: the publisher connects
to the leader of each partition at its advertised address, not only to the
bootstrap broker. Use IP literals (and IP\-literal advertised listeners): DNS
servers are not reachable from the unit. It conflicts with
.B robodr\-spool\-export.service
(one consumer per spool). On a diode site this program runs on the SOC side
with
.B \-\-diode\-out
instead, from the SOC's own unit or procedure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR robodr\-spool\-export (1),
.BR robodr\-diode\-unpack (1),
.BR robodr\-agentd (8),
/usr/share/doc/robodr/OPERATIONS.md
